WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.800
Welcome back to the Deep Dive. Today we are going to look at something that is sitting right in

00:04.800 --> 00:09.040
front of you. Maybe it's in your pocket, maybe it's on your desk. Right there on the desk.

00:09.360 --> 00:14.920
It's the screen you're looking at, the apps you use, the entire digital world that feels so

00:14.920 --> 00:22.020
incredibly simple. You tap a glass surface and a car shows up. You type a question and an answer

00:22.020 --> 00:31.720
appears. It feels frictionless. It feels like magic. But today we are going to shatter that

00:31.720 --> 00:36.880
illusion. It is a very comforting illusion, isn't it? The idea that we have mastered complexity.

00:37.180 --> 00:42.380
Yes. We've somehow tamed the chaos of the world. Exactly. We have this narrative, right? The

00:42.380 --> 00:48.380
triumphalist narrative of computer science. We tell ourselves that technology is the story of us

00:48.380 --> 00:53.800
conquering difficulty. We take impossible problems, we automate them, and poof, the difficulty is gone.

00:54.400 --> 01:00.940
But we've got a stack of papers here today that argue the exact opposite. They do. We are looking

01:00.940 --> 01:07.060
at two really dense, fascinating papers by an author named Flictioner. One is called Order and Obfuscation,

01:07.320 --> 01:12.700
and the other is The Mortality of Computation. And if I had to boil them down to a single,

01:12.700 --> 01:19.960
slightly terrifying sentence, it would be this. Computer science does not destroy difficulty.

01:20.880 --> 01:26.200
It just moves it around. The conservation of difficulty. That sounds like a law of physics.

01:26.600 --> 01:33.100
In many ways, it behaves exactly like one. Just like energy and physics, difficulty in computing

01:33.100 --> 01:38.800
is conserved. You can't create it, and you certainly can't destroy it. So you can only displace it.

01:38.800 --> 01:43.080
You can only displace it. You can push it down. You can push it sideways, or you can put it into

01:43.080 --> 01:46.840
the future. But it never, ever vanishes.

01:47.160 --> 01:49.900
Okay, hold on though. I want to push back on that immediately.

01:50.060 --> 01:50.560
Go for it.

01:50.740 --> 01:57.100
Because when I use a calculator to multiply massive numbers, or when I use Google Maps to find a route,

01:57.480 --> 02:02.840
that is objectively easier for me than doing it by hand. The difficulty for me is gone.

02:03.260 --> 02:03.620
Yes.

02:03.620 --> 02:05.820
So isn't that destroying difficulty?

02:06.040 --> 02:13.380
That is the crucial distinction. The difficulty for you, the user, is gone. But the difficulty of

02:13.380 --> 02:20.820
the task hasn't evaporated. It has been migrated. That smoothness you feel at the surface is purchased

02:20.820 --> 02:27.900
by burying a massive amount of turbulence somewhere else. And the scary part, which these papers argue,

02:27.900 --> 02:34.080
is that we are burying so much complexity, that the rug we're sweeping it under is starting to get

02:34.080 --> 02:35.220
very, very lumpy.

02:35.220 --> 02:40.900
I love that image, but I also find it kind of ominous. The lumpy rug of modern technology.

02:41.220 --> 02:41.600
Great metaphor.

02:41.920 --> 02:50.340
So our mission today is to unpack this architecture of concealment. We need to figure out if the difficulty

02:50.340 --> 02:53.700
isn't disappearing, where exactly is it going?

02:53.900 --> 02:54.100
Right.

02:54.100 --> 02:57.340
And what happens when the places we hide it get full?

02:57.920 --> 03:02.680
That is the multi-trillion dollar question. And it forces us to look at the architecture

03:02.680 --> 03:08.340
of our world not as a magic show, but as a waste management system for complexity.

03:08.580 --> 03:12.160
A waste management system. Wow. Okay, let's start with that rug metaphor,

03:12.380 --> 03:17.540
because fliction uses it right out of the gate in order and obfuscation. The quote is,

03:17.540 --> 03:23.500
The metaphor of sweeping problems under rugs captures this phenomenon. A rug does not remove

03:23.500 --> 03:26.120
what lies beneath it. It makes the surface navigable.

03:26.120 --> 03:33.540
Right. Think about walking across a floor. If there's debris everywhere, cables, dirt, unfinished

03:33.540 --> 03:36.500
floorboards, sharp nails, you can't walk.

03:36.640 --> 03:38.280
You trip. It's unusable.

03:38.460 --> 03:44.980
You trip. The rug makes the room usable. In computer science, we call the rug an interface.

03:44.980 --> 03:50.100
And the debris underneath is the complexity. But isn't that the whole point? I don't want

03:50.100 --> 03:53.060
to see the nails in the dirt. I just want to walk across the room.

03:53.060 --> 03:56.900
Absolutely. That is the utility of the rug. That's why we build interfaces.

03:57.620 --> 04:02.480
But here is the nuance that Flixson points out. It's not just ignoring the debris.

04:02.480 --> 04:08.540
Oh, okay. It's what the paper calls controlled reduction. You aren't just pretending the mess

04:08.540 --> 04:14.760
isn't there. You are actively engaging in a process to stabilize it just enough so you can

04:14.760 --> 04:17.440
put a lid on it. You have to do work to hide the work.

04:17.580 --> 04:20.340
It takes effort to hold the rug down. It's an active process.

04:20.680 --> 04:25.140
Okay, let's get specific. Because complexity is a buzzword we throw around a lot.

04:25.400 --> 04:26.400
It really is.

04:26.400 --> 04:29.340
I want to know what this debris actually looks like in a computer.

04:29.700 --> 04:32.740
Let's look at memory. The RAM in your computer.

04:32.880 --> 04:34.760
Okay. The short-term memory.

04:35.200 --> 04:41.360
To a modern programmer, or even to the user, memory feels like this infinite clean slate.

04:41.960 --> 04:47.820
You open a document. You save it. You declare a variable in code. It exists. It's an illusion

04:47.820 --> 04:48.460
of abundance.

04:48.640 --> 04:48.860
Sure.

04:48.860 --> 04:56.140
But physically, physically, memory is a nightmare. It is a chaotic mess of decaying electrical states.

04:56.340 --> 04:58.620
How so? I mean, it's just chips, right?

04:59.040 --> 05:06.100
It is charged states. Think about dynamic RAM or DRAM. It stores data as a tiny electrical charge

05:06.100 --> 05:09.640
in a capacitor. But capacitors are imperfect. They leak.

05:09.820 --> 05:10.940
They leak electricity.

05:10.940 --> 05:16.460
Constantly. If you just wrote data to RAM and walked away, the electrons would leak out in

05:16.460 --> 05:19.040
milliseconds, and your data would turn into random noise.

05:19.280 --> 05:21.740
So it's like writing on sand as the tide is coming in.

05:21.780 --> 05:26.220
That's a perfect analogy. So the hardware has to wake up thousands of times a second,

05:26.380 --> 05:31.660
literally thousands, and read every single cell, check its charge, and refresh it. It screams

05:31.660 --> 05:34.760
the data back to itself just to keep it from fading away.

05:34.760 --> 05:40.320
So while I'm sitting here staring at a static photo on my screen, thinking nothing is happening,

05:40.320 --> 05:44.560
my computer is frantically panic-refreshing billions of capacitors.

05:45.080 --> 05:50.800
Billions of them. In a constant state of managed panic. And that's just the physics.

05:51.180 --> 05:52.380
Then you have cosmic rays.

05:52.800 --> 05:55.420
Come on. Cosmic rays. Really?

05:55.540 --> 06:02.260
I'm serious. This is a real problem in systems engineering. High-energy particles from space

06:02.260 --> 06:06.840
occasionally hit a chip and flip a bit from a zero to a one.

06:06.960 --> 06:08.180
A single bit.

06:08.180 --> 06:14.640
A single bit. But if that happens in the wrong place in memory, your system crashes. Or worse,

06:14.820 --> 06:21.100
it gives you the wrong answer silently. So we have layers of error-correcting code, ECC,

06:21.500 --> 06:25.180
to detect that and silently fix it before you notice.

06:25.460 --> 06:27.040
And I see none of that.

06:27.140 --> 06:27.940
You see none of it.

06:28.060 --> 06:28.260
Yeah.

06:28.300 --> 06:31.940
Because there are layers. You have the hardware layer dealing with the voltage and the refresh

06:31.940 --> 06:32.460
cycles.

06:32.560 --> 06:32.800
Right.

06:32.800 --> 06:37.140
The firmware is dealing with the addressing. The operating system is managing the allocation,

06:37.580 --> 06:40.320
making sure two programs don't try to write to the same spot.

06:40.380 --> 06:42.400
And then even in the code itself, there are more layers.

06:42.600 --> 06:46.720
Exactly. The programming language has a garbage collector that runs around cleaning up memory

06:46.720 --> 06:47.820
you aren't using anymore.

06:47.940 --> 06:49.600
The janitor hidden under the road.

06:49.600 --> 06:56.280
Precisely. So Fliction formalizes this. The paper introduces two variables that I think are

06:56.280 --> 06:58.320
really helpful for visualizing this.

06:58.380 --> 06:58.620
Okay.

06:59.060 --> 07:03.780
You have eighth the explicit difficulty. That is the difficulty you experience.

07:04.000 --> 07:06.780
Which ideally is zero. I just click save.

07:06.900 --> 07:14.860
Right. But then there is hide the hidden difficulty. This is the structural complexity required to make

07:14.860 --> 07:19.620
that click work. The refreshing, the error correction, the garbage collection.

07:19.880 --> 07:20.480
Oh, the janitor.

07:20.640 --> 07:21.420
All the janitors.

07:21.560 --> 07:21.640
Yeah.

07:21.940 --> 07:27.120
And the conservation principle states that you can drive I down down, but you almost always

07:27.120 --> 07:33.720
have to increase I high dollar to do it. The total structural burden, call it sci-hiner,

07:34.720 --> 07:35.640
is conserved.

07:35.900 --> 07:41.080
Actually, Fliction argues the total burden usually goes up, doesn't he? It's not a one-to-one trade.

07:41.320 --> 07:42.640
Yes. That's a key point.

07:42.640 --> 07:47.340
Right. It's not a zero-sum game where one goes down and the other goes up by the same amount.

07:47.440 --> 07:48.660
It's not perfectly efficient.

07:48.900 --> 07:53.540
Not at all. Usually, to make something 10% simpler for the user, you have to make the

07:53.540 --> 07:55.780
system 50% more complex underneath.

07:56.020 --> 07:56.220
Right.

07:56.260 --> 07:59.980
You have to build the garbage collector, maintain the OS, design the error correction.

08:00.340 --> 08:02.480
You're adding new systems just to manage the old ones.

08:02.620 --> 08:10.160
So we are buying local tractability, making things easy right here, right now, by paying for

08:10.160 --> 08:11.680
it with global displacement.

08:11.680 --> 08:14.540
It's like cleaning your room by shoving everything to the closet.

08:15.020 --> 08:15.260
Yeah.

08:15.320 --> 08:16.200
The room looks clean.

08:16.320 --> 08:16.560
Yeah.

08:16.760 --> 08:18.480
The closet is a disaster zone.

08:19.140 --> 08:23.640
And eventually, you need a bigger closet, where you open it one day and everything falls

08:23.640 --> 08:24.100
on your head.

08:24.160 --> 08:27.540
But as long as you keep the closet door shut, the room works.

08:28.160 --> 08:30.380
And the paper makes a crucial point.

08:31.220 --> 08:32.940
This isn't laziness.

08:33.640 --> 08:34.680
This is necessary.

08:35.480 --> 08:36.420
Absolutely essential.

08:36.420 --> 08:42.560
If you had to think about voltage states every time you wanted to type a letter A, you effectively

08:42.560 --> 08:43.880
couldn't use the computer.

08:44.140 --> 08:44.960
We couldn't function.

08:45.380 --> 08:47.860
We have to hide the complexity to get anything done.

08:48.160 --> 08:49.800
That is the value of abstraction.

08:50.160 --> 08:50.340
Yeah.

08:50.520 --> 08:52.360
But the cost is opacity.

08:52.760 --> 08:53.240
Opacity.

08:53.600 --> 08:55.600
We lose touch with what is actually happening.

08:55.720 --> 08:58.400
We start to believe the world actually is that simple.

08:58.520 --> 09:00.100
We forget the closet is even there.

09:00.100 --> 09:05.860
This brings us to a really interesting part of the paper about quarantining impossibility.

09:06.220 --> 09:10.040
Because I always thought computers were these omnipotent logic machines.

09:10.300 --> 09:13.420
If you have enough processing power, you can solve anything.

09:14.080 --> 09:18.220
But Fliction says there are some things that are just impossible.

09:18.680 --> 09:20.600
This is where we get into the hard math limits.

09:21.500 --> 09:23.500
Computer science isn't just about engineering.

09:23.740 --> 09:25.280
It's about fundamental logic.

09:25.840 --> 09:27.760
There are monsters in the math.

09:28.440 --> 09:29.500
Monsters like what?

09:29.500 --> 09:30.940
Like the halting problem.

09:32.300 --> 09:34.820
Alan Turing proved this back in the 30s.

09:35.600 --> 09:37.300
He asked a simple sounding question.

09:38.180 --> 09:44.200
Can you write a program that looks at another program and determines with 100% certainty if

09:44.200 --> 09:46.600
it will run forever or eventually stop?

09:46.940 --> 09:48.640
Like an infinite loop detector.

09:48.820 --> 09:49.200
Exactly.

09:49.400 --> 09:50.540
A perfect debugger.

09:50.620 --> 09:51.780
And the answer is no.

09:52.000 --> 09:55.080
The answer is logically, mathematically, no.

09:55.740 --> 09:56.860
It is undecidable.

09:56.860 --> 10:01.660
You cannot build a universal debugger that predicts the future of code.

10:01.920 --> 10:05.460
It's a proven impossibility, like squaring a circle.

10:05.540 --> 10:10.340
So if that's impossible, how do we write software that doesn't just crash constantly?

10:10.600 --> 10:11.680
How is anything stable?

10:11.680 --> 10:14.400
We cheat, or rather we restrict.

10:14.800 --> 10:16.420
The paper calls it quarantining.

10:16.880 --> 10:21.420
We design our programming languages so that you simply aren't allowed to ask those questions.

10:21.540 --> 10:22.000
What do you mean?

10:22.200 --> 10:25.800
We use type systems that forbid certain recursive structures.

10:25.800 --> 10:37.320
We say, okay, we can't solve the halting problem for all programs, so we will only allow you to write a specific subset of programs where the problem is manageable or where we can prove it will halt.

10:37.540 --> 10:41.660
We build a wall around the scary parts of logic and say, don't go there.

10:41.980 --> 10:44.740
We create a safe walled garden to play in.

10:44.740 --> 10:45.420
Exactly.

10:46.180 --> 10:48.980
And that wall is another form of hidden complexity.

10:50.120 --> 10:52.640
The difficulty of the halting problem didn't vanish.

10:53.160 --> 10:57.140
It was displaced into the design of the type system itself.

10:57.340 --> 11:00.180
So the language designer had to take on that burden.

11:00.520 --> 11:09.020
The language designer had to grapple with that impossibility so that the application developer, the user, didn't have to face the abyss.

11:09.520 --> 11:13.520
They swept one of the scariest monsters in logic under the rug.

11:13.520 --> 11:19.480
This feels like it leads perfectly into the biggest magic trick of our current era, artificial intelligence.

11:19.560 --> 11:20.240
Oh, absolutely.

11:20.740 --> 11:27.020
Because if ever there was a rug that looked smooth on top but had a chaotic, unexplainable mess underneath, it's AI.

11:27.500 --> 11:27.860
Yes.

11:28.760 --> 11:32.760
The paper Order and Obfuscation dedicates a whole section to this.

11:33.380 --> 11:36.860
And it argues we are going through a massive paradigm shift.

11:36.860 --> 11:41.280
We are moving from symbolic systems to statistical systems.

11:41.760 --> 11:44.280
And this changes the nature of the difficulty we are hiding.

11:44.680 --> 11:45.560
Okay, break that down.

11:45.740 --> 11:47.360
Symbolic versus statistical.

11:48.100 --> 11:51.480
Because symbolic sounds like something from a Dan Brown novel.

11:51.920 --> 11:54.000
It's a bit less mysterious than that.

11:54.400 --> 12:02.540
Classical AI, I think 1980s, 90s, the stuff running in banking mainframes or early video games was symbolic.

12:02.760 --> 12:03.480
It was rules.

12:04.120 --> 12:04.440
Logic.

12:04.440 --> 12:06.220
If X, then Y.

12:06.380 --> 12:07.320
Like a flow chart.

12:07.480 --> 12:09.280
An incredibly complicated flow chart.

12:09.380 --> 12:09.820
Exactly.

12:10.020 --> 12:12.880
A giant, messy, human-made flow chart.

12:13.540 --> 12:17.360
If the patient has a fever and a cough, then check for flu.

12:18.080 --> 12:18.820
It was explicit.

12:18.960 --> 12:20.480
You could open up the code and see the reasoning.

12:20.640 --> 12:21.880
So the logic was transparent.

12:22.060 --> 12:22.700
It was transparent.

12:22.960 --> 12:26.160
The difficulty was visible because a human had to write every single rule.

12:26.520 --> 12:28.480
And if the rule was wrong, you could find it.

12:28.600 --> 12:32.100
So the explicit difficulty for the programmer was high.

12:32.520 --> 12:33.260
Very high.

12:33.260 --> 12:35.300
But the hidden difficulty was low.

12:35.420 --> 12:37.320
The machine wasn't doing anything mysterious.

12:37.320 --> 12:40.260
It was just following orders, just running down the flow chart.

12:40.640 --> 12:46.720
Now, compare that to modern machine learning, to ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude.

12:46.720 --> 12:48.680
They don't have rules.

12:48.880 --> 12:49.960
Not in the same way.

12:50.100 --> 12:51.320
They have parameters.

12:51.620 --> 12:51.960
Weights.

12:52.500 --> 12:53.180
Billions of them.

12:53.380 --> 12:54.700
Billions and billions of numbers.

12:55.120 --> 12:57.300
We call this parametric encapsulation.

12:57.820 --> 12:59.320
We stopped writing the rules.

12:59.920 --> 13:05.840
Instead, we built a massive statistical engine, a neural network, and fed it the entire internet.

13:05.980 --> 13:06.200
Right.

13:06.300 --> 13:08.160
Said, here is all the text in the world.

13:08.240 --> 13:09.420
You figure out the patterns.

13:09.420 --> 13:10.960
So where is the logic?

13:11.300 --> 13:12.440
Where is the reasoning?

13:12.960 --> 13:16.700
If I ask it a question and it gives me an answer, where did that answer come from?

13:16.980 --> 13:18.400
It is smeared.

13:18.700 --> 13:20.900
That is the word Flixson uses, and I think it's perfect.

13:21.780 --> 13:25.440
The reasoning isn't in a specific line of code.

13:25.680 --> 13:28.460
It is distributed across those billions of weights.

13:28.960 --> 13:32.540
It's a statistical tendency, not a logical rule.

13:32.540 --> 13:33.920
It's a pattern, not a reason.

13:34.120 --> 13:34.600
Exactly.

13:35.200 --> 13:39.180
The system has learned that statistically, these words tend to follow those words.

13:39.280 --> 13:43.160
The paper talks about the free lunch illusion here, because when I ask a chatbot a question,

13:43.280 --> 13:44.860
the answer comes back in seconds.

13:45.100 --> 13:46.180
It feels effortless.

13:46.640 --> 13:52.540
The explicit difficulty, what he calls infer of getting an answer, is near zero.

13:52.860 --> 13:53.780
I don't have to think.

13:53.980 --> 13:54.860
I just ask.

13:55.000 --> 13:55.900
It feels free.

13:56.060 --> 13:56.360
Right.

13:56.880 --> 13:58.740
But remember the conservation law.

13:58.740 --> 14:05.240
Now, if the inference, the answering part, is cheap for you, the cost must have gone somewhere else.

14:06.100 --> 14:11.960
And in AI, it went into two specific places that are dangerously opaque.

14:12.080 --> 14:12.280
Okay.

14:12.460 --> 14:13.020
Where did it go?

14:13.300 --> 14:15.660
First, it went into training energy.

14:16.100 --> 14:23.020
The massive, massive amount of electricity, water, and computation required to build that model in the first place.

14:23.440 --> 14:27.100
Some of these models cost hundreds of millions of dollars to train.

14:27.100 --> 14:29.060
We paid the energy bill up front.

14:29.240 --> 14:30.160
A huge bill.

14:30.700 --> 14:35.160
Paid up front to create the illusion of effortless intelligence later.

14:35.760 --> 14:39.060
So the difficulty was displaced in time, into the path.

14:39.160 --> 14:40.120
And the second place?

14:40.220 --> 14:41.640
The second place is structure.

14:42.380 --> 14:44.820
Specifically, overparameterization.

14:45.060 --> 14:45.900
Yeah, talk about that.

14:45.940 --> 14:48.620
I saw that term in the notes, and it sounds like bloat.

14:48.820 --> 14:50.720
Like we built it too big on purpose.

14:51.040 --> 14:52.120
It sounds like waste.

14:52.120 --> 14:55.440
But in this new paradigm, it's actually a requirement.

14:55.820 --> 14:57.140
It's a feature, not a bug.

14:57.540 --> 15:01.620
Imagine you need to pack for a trip, but you have absolutely no idea where you're going.

15:01.780 --> 15:02.540
It could be Antarctica.

15:02.880 --> 15:04.000
It could be the Sahara.

15:04.260 --> 15:05.720
It could be a formal dinner.

15:06.140 --> 15:07.220
I'd have to pack everything.

15:07.460 --> 15:10.260
Parka, swimsuit, tuxedo, a huge suitcase.

15:10.720 --> 15:11.120
Exactly.

15:11.300 --> 15:12.580
You need excess capacity.

15:13.500 --> 15:16.780
Modern AI models are overparameterized.

15:16.780 --> 15:23.960
They have way more parameters, way more brain cells than they technically need for any single task.

15:24.840 --> 15:27.300
This slack acts as a reservoir.

15:27.760 --> 15:30.140
It absorbs the complexity of the world.

15:30.520 --> 15:35.360
So the complexity of human language, all the slang, sarcasm, grammar, poetry,

15:35.360 --> 15:40.100
it all gets soaked up by this massive sponge of parameters.

15:40.160 --> 15:40.460
Yes.

15:40.820 --> 15:42.820
But because it's a sponge, it's unstructured.

15:43.360 --> 15:45.300
It's a displacement reservoir.

15:45.300 --> 15:51.280
The difficulty of understanding language hasn't been solved in a way we can write down as a rule.

15:51.540 --> 15:56.380
It has been dissolved into a high-dimensional geometry that humans can't visualize.

15:56.700 --> 15:58.780
We've buried the logic so deep we can't find it.

15:58.820 --> 16:00.040
We just know the machine works.

16:00.140 --> 16:00.960
Until it doesn't.

16:01.500 --> 16:02.960
And that is the risk of opacity.

16:03.620 --> 16:09.400
When a symbolic system fails, when the flowchart is wrong, you find the broken rule and fix it.

16:09.460 --> 16:10.200
You can debug it?

16:10.280 --> 16:11.000
You can debug it.

16:11.000 --> 16:18.000
When a statistical system fails, when it hallucinates or acts weirdly, it's because the hidden burden of the environment has shifted.

16:18.160 --> 16:19.080
Give me an example of that.

16:19.120 --> 16:20.500
What do you mean by the environment shifting?

16:20.960 --> 16:23.640
Well, the model was trained on data from the past.

16:24.160 --> 16:26.700
It assumes the future looks like the training data.

16:27.420 --> 16:30.300
Fliction calls this the hidden environment assumptions.

16:30.300 --> 16:41.440
If the context changes, say a new slang term emerges, a major world event happens, or the political climate shifts, the model doesn't just fail.

16:41.920 --> 16:43.560
It fails weirdly.

16:43.860 --> 16:47.460
It starts saying things that are out of date or just plain wrong.

16:47.460 --> 16:47.940
Exactly.

16:48.560 --> 16:55.320
And because the reasoning is smeared across billions of weights, fixing it isn't like fixing code.

16:55.460 --> 16:57.340
You can't just edit line 402.

16:57.460 --> 17:02.700
You have to retrain or fine-tune or hope it prompt engineering covers up the crack.

17:02.980 --> 17:09.340
It's like trying to fix a recipe when you've already baked the cake and you don't even know what the ingredients were because a robot bought them for you.

17:09.360 --> 17:11.400
It baked it in a black box you can't open.

17:11.640 --> 17:12.820
That is the trade-off.

17:12.820 --> 17:16.880
We've got immense capability, but we paid for it with interpretability.

17:17.460 --> 17:18.900
The difficulty didn't go away.

17:19.000 --> 17:19.840
It went to the shadows.

17:19.940 --> 17:20.940
This is unsettling.

17:21.260 --> 17:21.980
It really is.

17:22.060 --> 17:24.240
It makes me look at these AI tools differently.

17:24.600 --> 17:26.140
They aren't magic geniuses.

17:26.260 --> 17:28.740
They are just massive complexity sponges.

17:28.880 --> 17:31.420
Sponges that are incredibly expensive to keep wet.

17:31.720 --> 17:39.600
Which is the perfect segue to the second paper because order and obfuscation is mostly about the logical architecture, the code.

17:39.740 --> 17:40.040
Right.

17:40.200 --> 17:41.120
The software side.

17:41.120 --> 17:51.980
But the second paper, The Mortality of Computation, takes this existential dread and turns it up to 11 because it stops talking about code and starts talking about physics.

17:51.980 --> 17:59.620
This is where we have to leave the abstract world of logic and enter the messy, hot, degrading world of thermodynamics.

17:59.900 --> 18:04.600
The title alone, The Mortality of Computation, is a provocation.

18:04.940 --> 18:08.360
Because we have this bias, software is forever.

18:09.080 --> 18:10.640
Digital implies immortal.

18:11.160 --> 18:13.020
If I write a poem on paper, it burns.

18:13.120 --> 18:15.140
If I write it in the cloud, it's there forever.

18:15.580 --> 18:17.700
And Fliction says, wrong.

18:17.700 --> 18:21.420
The paper challenges the myth of immortality.

18:21.800 --> 18:30.660
It argues that logical universality, the idea that a computer can do anything, that a Turing machine is universal, does not equal physical immortality.

18:30.820 --> 18:32.520
Because the computer is made of stuff.

18:32.640 --> 18:33.400
It's not a ghost.

18:33.620 --> 18:37.060
It's made of stuff that degrades, stuff that obeys the laws of physics.

18:37.760 --> 18:40.420
Specifically, the second law of thermodynamics.

18:41.240 --> 18:41.640
Entropy.

18:41.980 --> 18:43.400
Disorder always increases.

18:44.120 --> 18:46.980
The universe wants to turn everything into lukewarm soup.

18:46.980 --> 18:47.580
Right.

18:47.940 --> 18:49.320
And here is the kicker.

18:49.640 --> 18:51.080
The point that connects everything.

18:52.080 --> 18:53.980
Computation is a physical process.

18:54.860 --> 18:59.700
We tend to think of it as math, happening in some platonic realm of pure thought.

19:00.260 --> 19:05.200
But every time a bit flips from zero to one, electrons move.

19:05.920 --> 19:06.840
Resistance happens.

19:07.340 --> 19:08.320
Heat is generated.

19:08.500 --> 19:10.500
The paper mentions Landauer's principle.

19:10.660 --> 19:15.120
I want to spend a minute here because this feels like the foundational law for this entire argument.

19:15.340 --> 19:15.680
It is.

19:15.680 --> 19:16.640
It's the bedrock.

19:16.980 --> 19:20.620
Rolf Landauer was a physicist at IBM in the 60s.

19:21.120 --> 19:23.980
He changed everything by proving that information is physical.

19:24.760 --> 19:31.320
Specifically, he proved that logically irreversible operations like erasing information must generate heat.

19:31.720 --> 19:31.980
Wait.

19:32.220 --> 19:33.940
Erasing information creates heat.

19:34.040 --> 19:35.940
That feels completely counterintuitive.

19:35.940 --> 19:41.680
I feel like writing information should take effort, but erasing it should be easy, like knocking over a sandcastle.

19:41.720 --> 19:42.580
That's what everyone thought.

19:42.780 --> 19:45.580
But think about it in terms of entropy or information.

19:46.580 --> 19:49.440
A bit that can be either zero or one is disordered.

19:49.800 --> 19:50.880
It has two possibilities.

19:51.100 --> 19:51.640
It's uncertain.

19:51.640 --> 19:57.600
If I reset that bit to a known state, say zero-one, I have forced it into a single state.

19:57.720 --> 19:58.720
I have created order.

19:58.860 --> 20:00.700
I have reduced the entropy of that bit.

20:01.160 --> 20:01.360
Okay.

20:01.440 --> 20:01.960
I'm with you.

20:02.280 --> 20:06.060
Organizing things, creating certainty, reduces local entropy.

20:06.060 --> 20:11.820
But the second law of thermodynamics says the total entropy of the universe must always increase.

20:12.300 --> 20:13.680
You cannot destroy entropy.

20:14.040 --> 20:15.380
You can only move it.

20:15.500 --> 20:15.580
Yeah.

20:15.760 --> 20:21.300
So if you reduce entropy inside the chip by organizing the data...

20:21.300 --> 20:22.740
You have to increase it somewhere else.

20:22.780 --> 20:25.900
You must increase entropy outside the chip to compensate.

20:26.260 --> 20:28.520
And how does the universe increase entropy?

20:28.940 --> 20:29.240
Heat.

20:29.740 --> 20:31.840
Heat is just disorganized energy.

20:31.840 --> 20:38.980
So to make that bit a clean, organized zer, the computer spits out a tiny puff of heat into the room.

20:39.420 --> 20:44.060
So my computer getting hot isn't just because the battery is inefficient or the fan is dusty.

20:44.280 --> 20:46.820
It's a fundamental law of the universe.

20:47.140 --> 20:49.900
To think, to compute, is to create heat.

20:50.100 --> 20:50.580
Exactly.

20:50.880 --> 20:54.800
You cannot process information without polluting the environment with heat.

20:55.500 --> 20:59.060
Fliction calls computation constrained energy descent.

20:59.180 --> 20:59.820
What does that mean?

20:59.820 --> 21:03.800
It means we are fighting a constant uphill battle against nature.

21:04.660 --> 21:05.780
Nature wants chaos.

21:06.020 --> 21:08.080
Nature wants those bits to flip randomly.

21:08.560 --> 21:09.560
To maintain a state.

21:10.000 --> 21:15.880
To keep your spreadsheet a spreadsheet and not a pile of random static requires a constant throughput of energy.

21:16.220 --> 21:20.300
We are paying energy to keep the structure from dissolving into noise.

21:20.440 --> 21:20.820
We are.

21:20.960 --> 21:24.200
The paper calls it an energy-hungry negotiation with physics.

21:24.200 --> 21:29.760
And this leads to the very practical, very expensive concept of the cooling problem.

21:30.160 --> 21:36.060
If you can't get that entropy, that heat, out of the system fast enough, the system crashes.

21:36.460 --> 21:37.080
It melts.

21:37.220 --> 21:37.620
It melts.

21:37.720 --> 21:41.080
Or it just stops working reliably because the signals get noisy.

21:41.080 --> 21:46.920
The entire architecture of a data center is just a massive machine for exporting entropy.

21:46.920 --> 21:51.560
So bringing this back to the rug, the rug isn't just hiding complex code.

21:51.720 --> 21:56.120
It's hiding the fact that our digital world is basically an entropy furnace.

21:56.380 --> 21:56.620
Yes.

21:57.080 --> 21:58.480
We see the smooth screen.

21:58.840 --> 22:01.880
We don't see the air conditioners humming on the roof of the data center.

22:02.100 --> 22:07.100
We don't see the coal or gas burning to power the electricity that fights the entropy.

22:07.100 --> 22:11.140
The hidden burden, the dollars, isn't just code complexity anymore.

22:11.460 --> 22:12.440
It's thermal load.

22:12.600 --> 22:13.280
It's physical.

22:13.840 --> 22:16.900
This connects to something the paper calls mortal computation.

22:17.540 --> 22:23.640
The idea that because we are embedded in this degrading physical substrate, software itself is mortal.

22:24.200 --> 22:25.780
It creates a minestable state.

22:26.160 --> 22:27.260
Do you know what metastable means?

22:27.660 --> 22:30.680
It sounds like kind of stable or stable for now.

22:30.780 --> 22:31.280
Pretty much.

22:31.540 --> 22:34.400
Imagine a ball balancing in the very peak of a mountain.

22:34.400 --> 22:38.120
It's staying there, but only if you actively balance it.

22:38.540 --> 22:42.100
The second you stop paying attention, it rolls down into the valley.

22:42.340 --> 22:42.600
Okay.

22:42.900 --> 22:44.180
That is metastable.

22:44.980 --> 22:46.580
A rock in a valley is stable.

22:46.860 --> 22:48.660
It stays there forever without effort.

22:49.500 --> 22:52.140
Our digital world is the ball on the mountain.

22:52.620 --> 22:56.320
It looks stable, but it's only stable as long as you pump energy into it.

22:56.460 --> 22:56.860
Wow.

22:56.860 --> 23:01.960
Stop the energy or let the hardware degrade too much and the abstraction collapses.

23:01.960 --> 23:05.720
The universal Turing machine is a mathematical ideal.

23:06.240 --> 23:09.080
The physical laptop on your desk is a dying machine.

23:09.440 --> 23:11.220
That is surprisingly dark.

23:11.440 --> 23:14.700
But it really reframes how we look at optimization.

23:15.520 --> 23:21.860
We always talk about optimizing code to make it faster or smaller, but the paper argues there are hard limits here too.

23:23.020 --> 23:24.060
Ontological friction.

23:24.300 --> 23:25.220
Ontological friction.

23:25.220 --> 23:26.180
I love that term.

23:26.400 --> 23:29.480
It suggests that resistance is built into the nature of existence.

23:29.480 --> 23:30.920
You cannot escape friction.

23:31.060 --> 23:32.540
It's not a bug you can fix.

23:32.800 --> 23:35.660
This gets into the P versus NP problem, right?

23:35.880 --> 23:39.520
Which is a famous unsolved problem in computer science.

23:39.860 --> 23:40.320
It does.

23:40.700 --> 23:42.960
We don't need to solve P versus NP today.

23:43.280 --> 23:44.760
That's a Nobel Prize waiting to happen.

23:45.200 --> 23:52.680
But the gist is, there are classes of problems that are easy to check but seem to be incredibly hard to solve.

23:52.800 --> 23:53.440
Like a puzzle.

23:53.740 --> 23:54.320
Like a puzzle.

23:54.320 --> 23:58.880
It's easy to look at a completed jigsaw puzzle and say, yes, that fits.

23:59.300 --> 24:03.020
It is incredibly hard to take a pile of pieces and build the picture.

24:03.580 --> 24:04.820
PE are the easy problems.

24:05.280 --> 24:07.500
NP are the hard ones that are easy to check.

24:07.620 --> 24:09.800
And the paper argues you can't cheat this.

24:09.900 --> 24:12.600
You can't just optimize away the difficulty of the puzzle.

24:12.820 --> 24:13.340
You can't.

24:13.620 --> 24:14.780
And there are other hard limits.

24:15.340 --> 24:16.700
Like space-time trade-offs.

24:17.080 --> 24:19.340
This is a classic computer science rule.

24:19.760 --> 24:20.960
You want to save time.

24:20.960 --> 24:23.840
You usually have to use more space, more memory.

24:24.080 --> 24:25.380
You want to save memory.

24:25.840 --> 24:28.800
You usually have to spend more time recalculating things.

24:29.120 --> 24:31.940
You can move the bump under the rug, but you can't flatten it.

24:32.040 --> 24:33.780
You can shift it from one place to another.

24:33.920 --> 24:34.320
Exactly.

24:35.160 --> 24:40.100
And in distributed systems, like the cloud, there's communication complexity.

24:41.060 --> 24:44.760
You can't eliminate the time it takes to coordinate between two servers.

24:45.240 --> 24:46.500
There's the speed of light.

24:46.820 --> 24:48.260
There's network latency.

24:48.260 --> 24:52.440
You can optimize it, but you hit a floor, a hard mathematical floor.

24:52.820 --> 24:55.940
So optimization is really just moving the cost around.

24:56.480 --> 24:57.900
We aren't erasing difficulty.

24:58.240 --> 25:03.120
We are just shifting it from time to space or from calculation to energy.

25:03.340 --> 25:06.980
Which brings us to the philosophical core of the mortality paper.

25:07.560 --> 25:10.960
This is where fiction brings in the philosopher Martin Heidegger.

25:11.400 --> 25:14.120
Which is a bold move for a computer science paper.

25:14.460 --> 25:17.260
Usually we stick to math, not German existentialism.

25:17.260 --> 25:20.420
It is bold, but it fits perfectly.

25:21.000 --> 25:23.960
Heidegger had this concept of in-framing or gestel.

25:24.480 --> 25:32.480
He argued that modern technology forces us to look at the world in a very specific, limited way as standing reserve.

25:32.800 --> 25:38.000
Standing reserve, meaning everything is just a resource waiting to be used, a raw material.

25:38.000 --> 25:38.620
Precisely.

25:39.020 --> 25:41.960
A tree isn't a tree with its own history and ecology.

25:42.280 --> 25:43.740
It's lumber waiting to be cut.

25:44.160 --> 25:45.260
A river isn't a river.

25:45.380 --> 25:47.720
It's hydroelectric power waiting to be spun.

25:48.140 --> 25:51.780
The world only exists to the extent that it can be input into our system.

25:51.960 --> 25:53.880
And in computing, how does that apply?

25:54.260 --> 25:57.720
In computing, Flexion argues we do this to everything.

25:57.720 --> 26:01.420
We treat the world purely as inputs and outputs.

26:01.740 --> 26:04.420
We see the interface, not the reality.

26:05.000 --> 26:08.300
We demand that the world fit into our database schema.

26:08.640 --> 26:10.620
If it doesn't fit, we ignore it.

26:10.820 --> 26:11.800
Or we force it.

26:12.000 --> 26:12.780
We abstract it.

26:12.780 --> 26:13.600
We abstract it.

26:13.760 --> 26:16.900
And this leads to what Heidegger called the supreme danger.

26:17.160 --> 26:18.220
The supreme danger.

26:18.220 --> 26:25.080
The supreme danger is the belief that the interface is the reality, that the map is the territory.

26:25.680 --> 26:30.000
We confuse the smooth, easy app with the reality of the messy world.

26:30.400 --> 26:33.680
We think because we can click order pizza, the pizza just appears.

26:33.920 --> 26:37.000
We forget the driver, the traffic, the oven, the fuel.

26:37.260 --> 26:38.860
And the interface totalization.

26:39.560 --> 26:42.780
We start to believe that everything can be wrapped in an API.

26:42.780 --> 26:49.360
That we can abstract away hunger or climate change or social complexity just by building

26:49.360 --> 26:50.080
a better interface.

26:50.320 --> 26:51.820
As if it's just a coding problem.

26:52.080 --> 26:55.280
We think if we just code it right, the difficulty vanishes.

26:55.880 --> 26:59.620
But the substrate, the physical reality underneath, is still there.

26:59.660 --> 27:00.680
And it is heating up.

27:00.880 --> 27:01.920
It is heating up.

27:02.000 --> 27:02.980
It is degrading.

27:03.120 --> 27:04.460
It is accumulating error.

27:05.460 --> 27:10.080
When we treat the world as inputs and outputs, we forget the cost of the processing.

27:10.260 --> 27:11.180
We forget the entropy.

27:11.180 --> 27:17.440
And that forgetting is dangerous because it makes us blind to the risks we are building up.

27:17.840 --> 27:22.100
This leads perfectly to the inevitable conclusion of all this hiding and displacing.

27:22.700 --> 27:23.760
The crash.

27:24.420 --> 27:25.680
The exposure event.

27:25.840 --> 27:27.400
The moment the rug gets lifted.

27:27.960 --> 27:29.700
Or the closet door bursts open.

27:29.980 --> 27:32.120
The paper redefines technical debt.

27:32.560 --> 27:37.600
I usually think of technical debt as, you know, I wrote sloppy code to meet a deadline and

27:37.600 --> 27:38.700
I'll fix it later.

27:38.980 --> 27:39.880
It's laziness.

27:39.880 --> 27:41.680
That's the surface definition.

27:42.020 --> 27:47.240
Fliction defines it much more profoundly as deferred explicit burden.

27:48.140 --> 27:50.260
Or my favorite, temporal displacement.

27:50.880 --> 27:53.140
Worrowing stability from the future.

27:53.300 --> 27:53.520
Yes.

27:53.940 --> 27:54.900
You make it easy now.

27:55.280 --> 27:59.600
You achieve local tractability by pushing the complexity into the future.

27:59.860 --> 28:02.740
You say, I'll assume this library will always work.

28:02.800 --> 28:05.460
Or I'll assume the user count won't go above a million.

28:05.460 --> 28:07.960
You hide the difficulty of scaling.

28:08.340 --> 28:13.620
You lower the explicit difficulty now by increasing the hidden difficulty of the future.

28:14.100 --> 28:14.640
Exactly.

28:14.920 --> 28:17.880
You're taking out a loan against future stability.

28:18.020 --> 28:19.600
And then the future arrives.

28:19.660 --> 28:20.560
And the bill comes due.

28:20.900 --> 28:22.280
That is the exposure event.

28:23.060 --> 28:29.020
It happens when the hidden burden, the highlander, accumulates to a point where the rug can't hold

28:29.020 --> 28:32.340
it down anymore or the environment changes.

28:32.660 --> 28:34.380
The paper calls this drift.

28:34.380 --> 28:38.260
The world changes, but your hidden assumptions stayed the same.

28:38.540 --> 28:39.000
Exactly.

28:39.360 --> 28:41.960
Maybe you assume the network would always be fast.

28:42.200 --> 28:44.680
Then you go mobile and the network is flaky.

28:45.360 --> 28:47.400
Suddenly that hidden assumption is exposed.

28:47.960 --> 28:48.960
The app crashes.

28:49.300 --> 28:50.500
The abstraction fails.

28:51.240 --> 28:54.400
The user is suddenly staring at a raw error code.

28:55.000 --> 28:56.020
The illusion breaks.

28:56.240 --> 28:58.380
Security vulnerabilities feel like this too.

28:58.520 --> 29:00.780
They feel like a classic exposure event.

29:00.780 --> 29:01.500
Perfectly.

29:01.780 --> 29:04.360
A security flaw is often just a hole in the abstraction.

29:04.620 --> 29:04.820
Yeah.

29:04.920 --> 29:07.680
You thought you were dealing with a high-level object, like a username.

29:08.440 --> 29:12.240
But a hacker found a way to manipulate the low-level memory underneath.

29:12.460 --> 29:13.640
They reached under the rug.

29:13.760 --> 29:16.820
They lifted the rug and crawled under it to steal your data.

29:17.280 --> 29:21.140
They exploited the gap between the clean interface and the messy reality.

29:21.140 --> 29:25.000
The paper talks about cascades in large systems.

29:26.000 --> 29:29.320
Why do these exposure events tend to ripple out?

29:29.460 --> 29:31.400
Why doesn't just one thing break?

29:31.740 --> 29:32.540
Coupling density.

29:33.500 --> 29:38.820
As we build these towers of abstraction, layer upon layer upon layer, everything gets connected.

29:39.680 --> 29:44.320
The app depends on the framework, which depends on the language, which depends on the OS,

29:44.680 --> 29:48.120
which depends on the cloud provider, which depends on the power grid.

29:48.300 --> 29:49.280
It's a Jenga tower.

29:49.280 --> 29:51.680
A Jenga tower where all the blocks are glued together.

29:52.140 --> 29:54.520
A small shake at the bottom can bring the whole thing down.

29:55.100 --> 29:57.480
The paper uses a term from control theory.

29:58.300 --> 29:59.300
Spectral radius.

29:59.500 --> 30:00.280
That sounds cool.

30:00.460 --> 30:01.320
Spectral radius.

30:01.700 --> 30:03.640
It is cool, but scary.

30:04.560 --> 30:09.400
Basically, it's a measure of how much a vibration amplifies as it moves through a system.

30:09.400 --> 30:15.720
If the system is too tightly coupled, a small failure in a hidden layer, say a single cloud

30:15.720 --> 30:21.920
server overheating in Virginia, a tiny local problem, can ripple upward, amplify at each

30:21.920 --> 30:26.320
layer of abstraction, and take down an entire airline booking system globally.

30:26.320 --> 30:31.480
Because we hid the failure modes, we made the interface look smooth so the upper layers

30:31.480 --> 30:33.020
didn't know how to handle a bump.

30:33.420 --> 30:35.620
They assumed the lower layers were perfect.

30:35.620 --> 30:36.640
Precisely.

30:37.040 --> 30:41.240
We designed for success, assuming the abstraction would hold.

30:41.920 --> 30:43.860
We didn't design for the abstraction breaking.

30:44.280 --> 30:48.260
We optimized for the best case scenario and ignored the worst case.

30:48.260 --> 30:53.280
Because looking at the worst case increases explicit difficulty.

30:53.680 --> 30:56.020
It makes the job harder right now.

30:56.440 --> 31:01.200
It's another form of temporal displacement pushing risks into the future.

31:01.200 --> 31:01.880
Yes.

31:02.160 --> 31:03.840
So, what is the solution?

31:04.080 --> 31:08.740
Are we doomed to just build bigger and more dangerous Jenga towers?

31:09.180 --> 31:12.600
The paper proposes something called conservation architecture.

31:12.960 --> 31:14.140
That is the proposal.

31:14.540 --> 31:15.780
And it starts with acceptance.

31:16.040 --> 31:17.320
It's a philosophical shift.

31:17.400 --> 31:18.620
You can't eliminate difficulty.

31:19.040 --> 31:22.540
So you have to design systems that expect the difficulty to migrate.

31:23.020 --> 31:25.320
You have to design knowing that the rug is lumpy.

31:25.440 --> 31:28.360
It sounds like admitting defeat, but it's actually just being realistic.

31:28.620 --> 31:29.220
It's maturity.

31:29.220 --> 31:34.560
It's moving from the triumphalist narrative, where we claim we solved it, to a conservation

31:34.560 --> 31:37.480
perspective, where we admit we're just managing it.

31:37.840 --> 31:38.980
So what does that look like in practice?

31:39.520 --> 31:42.800
It means designing interfaces that leak a little bit of truth.

31:43.180 --> 31:45.840
It means building in circuit breakers and bulkheads.

31:46.220 --> 31:51.680
It means monitoring the hidden burden, checking the closet to make sure it's not about to burst.

31:52.040 --> 31:54.260
It's about resilience, not perfection.

31:54.260 --> 32:00.360
And accepting that the system needs to breathe, it needs maintenance, it needs energy, it's

32:00.360 --> 32:00.960
not magic.

32:01.380 --> 32:02.140
It is not magic.

32:02.540 --> 32:03.420
It is engineering.

32:03.960 --> 32:05.520
And engineering is about trade-offs.

32:05.660 --> 32:09.020
It's about managing complexity, not pretending it doesn't exist.

32:09.220 --> 32:10.780
Let's try to synthesize all of this.

32:11.100 --> 32:12.380
We've covered a lot of ground.

32:12.520 --> 32:15.240
We have these two massive papers by Fliction.

32:15.240 --> 32:19.920
What is the big picture here, the new ontology that Papel talks about?

32:20.300 --> 32:24.200
I think it comes down to a unified structural ontology that has three parts.

32:24.580 --> 32:27.360
It's like a three-step syllogism for our digital age.

32:27.780 --> 32:29.680
Number one, universality.

32:29.900 --> 32:31.100
We can compute anything.

32:31.480 --> 32:32.940
The promise of Turing is real.

32:33.260 --> 32:35.580
We can represent any process as code.

32:35.840 --> 32:36.640
We have the potential.

32:36.800 --> 32:37.520
And there's a price.

32:37.640 --> 32:38.460
There's always a price.

32:38.460 --> 32:41.020
Number two, irreversibility.

32:42.220 --> 32:45.620
Every time we do that computation, we are bound by physics.

32:46.020 --> 32:47.080
We generate entropy.

32:47.580 --> 32:49.580
We pay a cost in energy and heat.

32:50.000 --> 32:51.500
We cannot cheat Landauer.

32:51.940 --> 32:53.040
It is an iron law.

32:53.520 --> 32:56.080
And number three, the conclusion.

32:57.340 --> 32:58.380
Metastable order.

32:59.360 --> 33:03.680
The result isn't a permanent, immortal, abstract perfection.

33:03.680 --> 33:11.480
It is a temporary, fragile stability that we have to actively maintain against the flow of the universe.

33:11.800 --> 33:13.440
It's the ball on top of the mountain.

33:13.980 --> 33:16.900
Smoothness at the surface equals turbulence underneath.

33:17.160 --> 33:17.440
Always.

33:18.140 --> 33:20.560
The smoother the ride, the harder the engine is working.

33:20.900 --> 33:25.060
The more magical the interface, the more frantically the janitors are working in the basement.

33:25.400 --> 33:28.100
So here is my final thought for the listener.

33:28.100 --> 33:33.600
We started by looking at that screen in your pocket, that smooth glass interface.

33:33.920 --> 33:35.000
The magic glass.

33:35.220 --> 33:36.800
I want you to look at it differently now.

33:36.900 --> 33:38.400
I want you to see it as a battleground.

33:38.540 --> 33:39.820
The battleground against physics.

33:40.700 --> 33:43.320
Against the second law of thermodynamics itself.

33:43.580 --> 33:51.320
Every streamlined app, every effortless AI answer, every instant transaction, it's all silently accumulating hidden entropy.

33:51.960 --> 33:53.800
It's building up structural debt.

33:53.880 --> 33:55.020
It's heating up the planet.

33:55.020 --> 33:58.380
It is sweeping debris under a rug that is running out of room.

33:58.860 --> 33:59.820
It raises the question.

33:59.820 --> 34:06.380
We are building a world of mortal computation that masquerades as immortal.

34:07.460 --> 34:13.500
We are stacking deeper and deeper layers of abstraction, assuming the foundation will hold forever.

34:13.500 --> 34:21.080
But if the conservation of difficulty is real, if the debt is always conserved, when is the bill due?

34:21.500 --> 34:23.540
And are we solvent enough to pay it when it comes?

34:24.100 --> 34:27.600
Something to think about the next time your phone gets a little warm in your hand.

34:27.940 --> 34:29.700
That heat isn't a bug.

34:30.060 --> 34:31.420
It's the cost of thinking.

34:31.740 --> 34:33.160
It's the friction of reality.

34:33.420 --> 34:34.800
Thanks for diving deep with us.

34:34.920 --> 34:35.760
We'll see you next time.

